title: Mass operations in the log
tags: Storage [[Event log]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: A write adding a value to, removing it from, or replacing a field of many records, or deleting them, is one entry in the log, which reads as one operation per record; nothing is read or written per record.

A write that adds one value to a field of ten thousand records, or removes it, used
to read each record to log what it held and to write one operation, its two snapshots
and three index keys for it. It is now one entry, a ''stub'' in `ops`: the field, the
value, and the records that ''changed'' — those that lacked the value, or held it.
Which ones those are comes from the holders of the value ([[Mass writes in the store]]),
not from the records.

The write names its records by ''dense id'': the query ends with a bitmap, and the
write takes it as it is (`Writer::mass_add_ids`, `mass_remove_ids`; the routes of
`field add` and `field remove` and the batch endpoint). Nothing is read per record —
no uuid to resolve, no record, no existence check — so the keys a write reads do not
depend on the number of records (`tests/kv_mass_log.rs` counts them). The stub keeps
the ''ids'' (a bitmap); a reindex, which numbers the ids again, first turns every stub
into the uuids of its records, in the order of its ids, so an operation keeps its
record. A call that has uuids (`mass_add`) looks their ids up, once each.

A stub holds as many operation ids as it names records: the ids that follow its key.
What reads the log is given, for the id `first + k`, the operation a write to the
`k`-th record alone would have logged — `append_field` with the row after it, or
`delete_field` with the row before it — made up on reading, so a revert, the history,
the change feed and the trash correlation know nothing of stubs. Ids are still
one per record and never reused; a write to many records moves HEAD by the number
of records it changed.

|!Where |!A stub |
|`ops` |one key, at its first id; an operation's header, then the row and the records |
|`ops_by_label` |one key, whose value is the last id (an operation's is empty) |
|`ops_by_entity` |one key under the nil uuid, which lists the stubs |
|`snaps` |nothing |
|`watcher_ops`, `user_ops` |one key |

A metarecord's operations after an id are its own and, for each stub newer than the
id, its place among the stub's records: a search over the stubs after the id,
not over every record's keys. A stub is read once for the operations it holds, which
`ops_of` and the backward read keep.

!! A replacement

`field set` and `field unset` on a query log one stub too, with the values that
replaced the rows (none for `unset`). Its operations read as `set_field` (or
`delete_field` for `unset`, of the records that held the field). A replacement forgets
what it replaces, so the stub keeps it: for each value the field held on the records,
the ''places'' — among the stub's records — of those that held it, a `Nothing` row
included. They are places, not ids, so a reindex has nothing to rewrite in them. The
write finds them without a record per id: the postings of the field, the rows of the
values that have none, the events pending ([[Mass writes in the store]]), and one
record per ''distinct value'' found, to give the value back (a key is not always its
value: an int and a float of one number share it). The cost is that of the field's
vocabulary — the number of its distinct values and of its rows outside a posting —
not of the records written. The types' counts (`field_types`) are kept from the
counters: the rows that go are the sum of the field's counts over the records.

!! A deletion

Deleting many records (`POST /repos/:repo/query/delete`) is one stub of type
`mass_delete`, read as a `delete_metarecord` per record. The records are ''not''
touched: their ids leave the universe, which every answer is sifted through
([[Deleting a metarecord in the store]]), and that is all the write does to them. The rows each
held are still in its key, which is where the log reads them while the deletion is
''pending'' — a mark in the `mass` table, under a part of its own (`DELETES_PART`), with
the ids — and what an undo gives back. Until the fold the key of a uuid in `metarecords`
and the record stay; reads that find them sift them through the universe (`exists`, the
count and the list of metarecords, a record's rows), and only while a deletion is pending
(`KvStore`'s `deletes` flag), so a store with none reads as before.

What cannot be sifted is kept exact at the write, without reading a record: the type
counts (a sum over the counters of every field), and the parents that lost their last
child (one sequential pass over the referrers of each position field, the writes the
transaction made before read as they stand). The histograms count rows and are not:
they give the rows back at the fold, and `mf repo check` folds first.

A folder deleted with every record under it is deleted so, whatever the depth: all of them
leave the universe together, and the dead ids that stay in its ancestors' descendants
are sifted like any other. A folder that keeps a child is not — the children would be
left under a node that is gone, which the commit refuses for a `tree_ref`, and their
subtree would leave its ancestors' descendants, which no universe sifts — so the records
are deleted one by one, as before.

The fold ([[Folding in idle time]]) takes the deletions ''first'', before any part: it
reads each record with the entries of its part still applied, writes its rows into the
snapshots of its operation (so the log reads them there from then on), gives the rows
back to the histograms and deletes the record and its key in `metarecords` — unless the
uuid has a metarecord again by then (an undo before the fold), which it leaves be.

!! Pruning and retention

A prune that names an id inside a stub cuts it: the older operations go, the stub
begins at the id (a stub's operations are removed oldest first, never from its
middle). Retention counts a stub as one operation of its class and takes it whole
when it is among the oldest. A mass write is one unit of history, as it is one
command; the bound on the log is the bound on its entries.

!! The schema check

A write by id is checked against the schema ''once'': the indexes say which records
could violate a constraint on the field — a row of the wrong type, too many rows, too
few, none where a type requires one ([[Schema constraints]]) — and those among the records the write
changed are validated as a write to one record is. A repository with no violation has
next to no such record, whatever the size of the write; one with many pays for
them (the question is not restricted to the changed records).

!! What is not here yet

* the history of a metarecord is a search over the stubs after an id: a summary per
  block of operations is the next step if the number of stubs asks for it;
`tests/kv_mass_log.rs` holds the reads of a stub to those of the operations it stands for.
